.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-2-Clause
.Dd October 22, 2026
.Dt FIDL 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm fidl
.Nd experimental Fuchsia Interface Definition Language integration
.Sh DESCRIPTION
FIDL defines service protocols in schemas and generates language bindings
for encoding, decoding, and dispatching messages.
The native 5BSD backend combines upstream natural types and codecs with
.Xr libchannel 3 .
Generated C++ adapters and a generated C ABI use BSD capability descriptors
as attachments while retaining kernel enforcement of their authority.
.Ss Runtime
The compiler and generators run at build time.
Their output uses library support while a client or server runs;
this is the FIDL runtime.
Generated code supplies protocol-specific types and method handling.
Shared code supports validation, request and reply tracking, transport,
dispatch, and resource cleanup.
On 5BSD these operations use native channels, descriptors, and kqueue.
No Zircon kernel, syscall emulation, Fuchsia dispatcher, or central routing
daemon is needed by this backend.
.Ss Status
As of October 22, 2026, the compiler, formatter, linter, C++ and Rust
generators, native C++ runtime, and generated C bridge build on 5BSD, and
buildworld
generates every binding the system uses from the schemas in the tree.
The native backend supports the schema subset the system's providers use.
Fourteen of the fifteen capability daemons serve FIDL while preserving their
public C client library ABIs: BSDTime, BSDPower, BSDDevice, BSDTrace,
BSDSysctl, BSDCrypto, BSDAudit, BSDNetwork (system.Network, .Admin, .State
and, since October 22, .PF), BSDNotify, BSDLog, BSDVM, BSDNamespace,
BSDExtension and BSDFilesystem (with system.OES).
BSDBluetooth is not converted; BSDMemory serves no endpoint.
The fifteen production libraries are versioned under the FIDL platform
.Dq bsd
(see
.Sx Versioning ) .
Plane-on VM validation of the converted daemons is recorded in
.Pa /usr/src/tools/build/fuchsia/VALIDATION.json
.Pq Li fidl_daemons ;
the last two conversions (system.Network.PF and the versioning change) carry
their own records there.
Rust runtime bindings remain unported.
.Pp
Remaining work is listed in priority order in
.Pa /usr/src/tools/build/fuchsia/REMAINING.md :
verification of the committed tree in isolation, quiet-host latency
measurement, a typed descriptor schema library, build-time message size
checks, a schema ABI gate at publication, persistent encoding, generated
protocol documentation, the BSDBluetooth conversion and Rust bindings.
.Ss Versioning
Every production library is declared
.Ql @available(platform="bsd", added=1) .
The library name's
.Ql .vN
suffix is the wire major: ordinals and layouts are a stable contract and an
incompatible change is a new library name.
The platform version
.Ql bsd:N
is the additive level shared by all
.Ql bsd.*
libraries: an element added later is marked
.Ql @available(added=N)
with the next level.
.Xr fidlc 1
refuses a versioned library without a selected version, so every compile
passes
.Fl -available Ar bsd:N :
.Pa lib/libfidl/fidl-bindings.mk
compiles each binding set at
.Va FIDL_AVAILABLE
.Pq default Ql bsd:1 ,
an evolution test's next schema selects
.Ql bsd:2
through
.Va FIDL_FLAGS.NAME ,
and a schema installed under
.Pa /usr/share/fidl
is compiled by hand with
.Ql --available bsd:1 .
Peers built at different levels interoperate under FIDL's unknown-interaction
rules; see
.Pa /usr/src/lib/libfidl/README
.Pq Versioning .
.Pp
Use existing typed client libraries and
.Xr libservice 3
for production applications.
Native backend contracts and supported schemas are described in
.Xr libfidl_bsd 3
and
.Pa /usr/src/lib/libfidl/README .
.Ss Relationship to current IPC
Current services define wire structures and validate their message fields
and descriptor slots explicitly.
FIDL can replace repeated protocol definitions and parsing with schemas
and generated bindings.
It does not provide application authorization or delivery guarantees.
.Pp
The existing asynchronous calls, bounded queues, cancellation, and native
lifecycle machinery of
.Xr libchannel 3
support the new adapters.
The discovery and admission responsibilities of
.Xr libservice 3
and
.Xr switchboard 8
remain in place.
Importing FIDL does not supply Fuchsia's Component Manager or service namespace.
.Pp
Measured on October 22, 2026: FIDL C makes no heap allocations per call on
either side, matching raw
.Xr libchannel 3 ,
and a synchronous call makes one system call on a kernel that reports
.Dv MAC_CAPABILITY_INFO_F_CHANNEL_CALL
(two for split send and receive on that kernel, four on older kernels).
No wall-clock advantage over the hand-written protocols has been measured on
a quiet host; see
.Pa /usr/src/tools/build/fuchsia/BENCH.md .
The expected benefit remains protocol maintenance and shared validation.
Costs include the binding runtime, build dependencies, upstream maintenance,
and preserving the C API boundary.
.Sh CAPABILITY SEMANTICS
Native channel attachments carry descriptors; integers in payload bytes
cannot designate authority in another process.
The backend checks message shape, handle counts, native types and rights,
and closes unclaimed attachments on rejection or shutdown.
Descriptor zero is valid.
READ and WRITE constraints have explicit Capsicum mappings.
The kernel continues to enforce transfer ceilings, revocation, and locked
exec and fork restrictions.
.Pp
FIDL transaction IDs remain separate from native 64-bit reply tokens.
Kernel sender and software-authority metadata remains attached to the request.
Providers must still authorize operations using held capabilities;
a client-supplied identity or method name grants no authority.
.Sh LIMITATIONS
Rust runtime bindings remain an implementation gap.
The native C/C++ bindings support closed, ajar, and open protocols, including
flexible interactions; see
.Xr libfidl_bsd 3
for unknown-method and event behavior.
Zircon-only object types require a real native capability contract before
being supported.
Unsupported schemas and mappings fail closed.
.Sh VALIDATION
The initial compiler run passed 2,203 enabled tests with four upstream tests
remaining disabled; generator tests also passed.
The native runtime suite passes 66 tests, a C11 client/server integration
test, and 36 generator contract checks.
Eight dependency-graph checks and a three-library C round trip also pass,
including generation and compilation with extracted pkgbase SDK files.
ASan and UBSan pass these suites.
LeakSanitizer is unavailable on this host; descriptor leaks are checked
explicitly through native pipe lifetime tests.
The schema evolution test compiles one schema at
.Ql bsd:1
and
.Ql bsd:2 ,
proves the elements added at 2 are absent from the
.Ql bsd:1
bindings and present at 2, and runs the separately built C peers against each
other in every pairing;
.Pa tools/build/fuchsia/test-bindings.py
generates every binding set twice and proves each provider's next schema is
its production schema at
.Ql bsd:1 .
.Pp
With
.Va MK_TESTS ,
the native build installs tests and a Kyuafile under
.Pa /usr/tests/lib/libfidl ,
owned by the libfidl-tests package.
The C++ runtime suite also requires
.Va MK_GOOGLETEST .
The installed static tests need no host compiler or source checkout.
Run them with Kyua on a matching 5BSD kernel.
The
.Cm check-native
target in
.Pa tools/build/fuchsia
runs native, sanitizer, SDK and extracted-package checks.
.Pp
Each converted daemon is validated in a plane-on VM with its provider,
client, control-tool and evolution suites
.Pq Pa tools/build/fuchsia/vm-provider.sh ;
the records are in
.Pa tools/build/fuchsia/VALIDATION.json .
Standalone backend tests do not establish a completed service migration;
BSDBluetooth remains on its record protocols.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Pa /usr/src/contrib/fuchsia
Selected upstream sources and notices.
.It Pa /usr/src/lib/libfidl
Native backend, C bridge, contracts, and tests.
.It Pa /usr/src/tools/build/fuchsia
Build and generation tools, pinned import manifest, local patches, and
validation records.
.It Pa /usr/src/docs/book/src/develop/fidl.md
Comparison with current IPC, runtime explanation, and migration status.
.El
